No, the official Prehistoric-Wildlife.com website does not have a public, official API. [1, 2] 
While certain pages on their site display error messages like "Could not load fossil data. The API might be down", this refers strictly to internal maps pulling spatial data from external scientific sources, rather than a data feed they open up to outside developers. [3, 4, 5] 
If you are building an automated script or a database connection to feed a content pipeline, you have three alternative options to use instead:
## 1. The Official Science Route: The Paleobiology Database (PBDB)
If you want deep, accurate, and completely free scientific data, use the [Paleobiology Database API](https://paleobiodb.org/). [6] 

* The Vibe: This is the massive, global open database used by actual university researchers and field paleontologists.
* How to use it: They have a completely public, highly detailed REST API. You can send a request for a specific species (like http://paleobiodb.org) and it will return a massive JSON file detailing the exact geological era, coordinates of where it was dug up, full taxonomic hierarchy, and body mass estimates. [6, 7] 

## 2. The Simple Coder Route: Write a Custom Python Scraper
Because Prehistoric-Wildlife's data is organized using incredibly clean, alphabetized HTML index structures (e.g., /species/titanoboa/ or /species/vasuki/), it is one of the easiest sites on the internet to web-scrape.

* How to do it: You can use a basic Python script pairing requests and BeautifulSoup to target their alphabetical index pages.
* What you can extract: You can configure the script to systematically loop through their indices, grab the text underneath standard headers like Diet:, Size:, and Known locations:, and dump them straight into a tidy CSV spreadsheet or JSON file to feed your LLM video scriptwriter. [8] 

## 3. The Pre-Made Github Route: Dinosaur APIs
If you don't want to scrape or parse raw scientific geology data, indie developers on GitHub have already done the heavy lifting for general dinosaur profiles.

* Where to look: Open-source projects like the [dinoapi repository on GitHub](https://github.com/darkmoonsk/dinoapi) provide pre-built, light APIs specifically populated with classic dinosaur dimensions, feeding habits, and timeline statistics. [2] 

